{{also|synæsthesia}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2019|May|1}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|grc|σύν||with}} + {{m|grc|αἴσθησις||sensation}}, modelled after {{m|en|anaesthesia}}. It is analysable as {{prefix|en|syn|aesthesia}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˌsɪn.ɪsˈθiː.ʒə/|/ˌsɪn.ɪsˈθiː.zɪ.ə/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˌsɪn.əsˈθi.ʒə/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|iːʒə|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|syn|aes|thes|ia|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|s|synaesthesiae}}

# {{lb|en|neurology|psychology}} A [[neurological]] or [[psychological]] [[phenomenon]] whereby a particular [[sensory]] [[stimulus]] [[trigger#Verb|triggers]] a second kind of [[sensation]]. {{defdate|from late 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Campbell Young|title=An Experimental Study of Mental and Physical Functions in the Normal and Hypnotic States: Additional Results|editors=[[w:Margaret Floy Washburn|Margaret F[loy] Washburn]], {{w|Karl M. Dallenbach}}, [[w:I. Madison Bentley|[Isaac] Madison Bentley]], and Edward G. Boring|journal=[[w:American Journal of Psychology|The American Journal of Psychology]]|location=Ithaca, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Cornell University}}|month=July|year=1926|volume=XXXVII|section=footnote 6|page=346|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=vDMwAAAAYAAJ&pg=RA11-PA346|issn=0002-9556|oclc=317911194|passage={{...}} R. H. Gault's case of '''synesthesia''' in a deaf and blind girl, who could distinguish colors by the sense of smell, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Julian Blackburn|chapter=Introduction|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hcJdAgAAQBAJ&pg=PT38|title=Psychology and the Social Pattern|series=International Library of Sociology and Social Reconstruction|seriesvolume=251|location=London|publisher=[[w:Routledge|Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner & Co.]]|year=1945|oclc=953154651|location2=London|publisher2={{w|Routledge}}|year2=2013|isbn2=978-0-415-17790-0|passage=Now just as normal perceptions in terms of sense organs different from those stimulated can be interpreted through common past experiences, so it is likely that '''synæsthesias''' may be partly explicable in terms of individual experiences.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Gibson|authorlink=William Gibson|chapter=18|title={{w|Neuromancer}}|series=[[w:Sprawl trilogy|Sprawl]]|seriesvolume=book 1|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Ace Books}}|date=1 July 1984|page=221|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/neuromancer00gibs#page/221/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-441-56959-5|passage=Into her darkness, a churning '''synaesthesia''', where her pain was the taste of old iron, scent of melon, wings of a moth brushing her cheek.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sean A. Day|chapter=What Synaesthesia Is (and Is Not)|editors=Paul Mc Kevitt, Seán Ó Nualláin, and Conn Mulvihill|title=Language, Vision and Music: Selected Papers from the 8th International Workshop on the Cognitive Science of Natural Language Processing, Galway, Ireland 1999|series=Advances in Consciousness Research|seriesvolume=35|location=Amsterdam; Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher={{w|John Benjamins Publishing Company}}|year=2002|page=171|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OyRZAQL0x6QC&pg=PA171|isbn=978-90-272-5155-8|issn=1381-589X|passage=For example, I myself have a type of '''synaesthesia''': The sounds of musical instruments will sometimes make me see colors, about a yard in front of me, each color specific and consistent with the particular instrument playing.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Adam Mars-Jones|authorlink=Adam Mars-Jones|chapter=Permission to Die|title=[[w:Pilcrow (novel)|Pilcrow: Novel]]|location=London|publisher={{w|Faber and Faber}}|year=2008|isbn=978-0-571-21703-8|edition2=1st paperback|location2=London|publisher=Faber and Faber|year2=2009|page2=231|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=kk1Pqh4ITC0C&pg=PA231|isbn2=978-0-571-21704-5|passage=For me Saturday was a bright red color, just as the other days of the week were chromatically coded. {{...}} This wasn't the true '''synæsthesia''' which is such a fascinating mystical hint, a loose thread in the fabric of perception left a-dangle, an unravelling which suggests that we could dissolve all our unreal categories.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Helena Melero|chapter=From Pain to Pleasure: A Neuroscientific Approach to Pain-color and Orgasm-color Synesthesias|editors=M[ari]a José de Córdoba Serrano, Julia López de la Torre Lucha, and Timothy B. Leiden|title=Actas del ‘VI Congreso Internacional de Sinestesia, Ciencia y Arte + Actividades Paralelas 2018’: Alcalá la Real, Jaén (España)|location=Granada, Spain|publisher=Fundació Internacional Artecittà|month=April|year=2018|page=4|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gihZDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA10-IA47|column=1|isbn=978-84-948665-0-0|passage=[S]ynesthetes themselves can provide the indispensable cues to design a structured qualitative questionnaire: color '''synesthesias''' and their variations during sexual arousal, direct physical stimulation, and orgasm {{...}} can help scientists differentiate the specific triggers and their characteristics.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Graham Richards|chapter=|title=Psychology: The Key Concepts|series=Key Concepts|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Routledge}}|year=2009|page=244|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_p_DYj_UdKcC&pg=PA244|isbn=978-0-415-43200-9|passage='''Synaesthesia''' can occur particularly powerfully during mescalin and LSD intoxication, and is often given mystical significance.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} The [[association]] of one sensory [[perception]] with, or [[description]] of it in terms of, a different perception that is not [[experience#Verb|experienced]] at the same time.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Claude Lévi–Strauss|authorlink=Claude Lévi–Strauss|author2=Claire Jacobson and Brooke Grundfest Schoepf, transl.|chapter=Postscript to Chapters III and IV|title=Structural Anthropology: Translated from the French|series=Harper Torchbooks|seriesvolume=TB5017|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Basic Books}}|year=1963|pages=91–92|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=2xaB9PksH1UC&pg=PA91|oclc=490234036|passage=On a phonemic level, phenomena of '''synesthesia''' have often been described and studied. Practically all children and a good many adults—though for the most part adults will deny it—spontaneously associate sounds, whether phonemes or the timbre of musical instruments, with colors and forms.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Boris Wiseman|chapter=Structuralism, Symbolist Poetics and Abstract Art|title=Lévi-Strauss, Anthropology, and Aesthetics|series=Ideas in Context|seriesvolume=85|location=Cambridge|publisher={{w|Cambridge University Press}}|year=2007|page=112|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pOJTWmu9rlMC&pg=PA112|isbn=978-0-521-87529-5|passage=For one of the enigmatic features of '''synaesthesia''' is that, within a given cultural group, the kinds of associations made by different subjects occur according to statistically verifiable recurring patterns. As Jakobson explains, ‘when we ask whether /i/ or /u/ is darker, testing such phonic oppositions as grave vs. acute, some of the subjects may respond that this question makes no sense to them, but hardly one will respond that /i/ is the darker of the two’{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|arts|literature}} A [[literary]] or [[artistic]] [[device]] whereby one kind of sensation is described in the terms of another.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Stephen Bowkett|authorlink=Stephen Bowkett|chapter=Descriptive Writing|title=Boys and Writing|series=Pocket PAL|location=London|publisher=Network Continuum Education, {{w|Continuum International Publishing Group}}|year=2006|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DGxa0WXX2B4C&pg=PT39|isbn=978-1-85539-108-6|passage=Linking moods with colours is one example of '''synaesthesia'''. {{...}} As a tool for improving writing, the idea of '''synaesthesia''' is versatile and innovative. Why use only visual references to describe visual impressions? Why just describe sounds using sound-related words? Play with cross-matching the senses.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Antti-Ville Kärjä|chapter=Visions of a Sound Nation: Finnish Music Videos and Secured Otherness|editors=Roger Beebe and Jason Middleton|title=Medium Cool: Music Videos from Soundies to Cellphones|location=Durham, N.C.; London|publisher={{w|Duke University Press}}|year=2007|page=181|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8f_-1giPRFoC&pg=PA181|isbn=978-0-8223-4139-0|passage=[I]t may be stated that the concept of '''synaesthesia''' is instrumental for understanding music videos, since videos are based on the soundtrack's visual associations. Yet to discuss associations here may cause some problems. {{...}} &#91;{{w|Nicholas Cook}}&#93; seems to be relying on a rather normative conception of '''synaesthesia''', which for its part supports the idea of absolute, nonrepresentational music. The implication of his discussion is that the "true" forms of music–image '''synaesthesia''' come only in the forms of perception of color or light when hearing a certain musical sound.}}

